INT. COUNTY COURTHOUSE - COURTROOM 3 - NIGHT

The room after everyone has gone. Folding chairs askew, a fan turning lazily above empty benches, a bailiff's coffee cup still ringing the oak rail. The jury box is a row of pressed-flat cushions.

RHEA KOSTOPOULOS, 40s, defense attorney, sits alone at the long table with her shoes off and her jacket over the chair. The verdict form lies in front of her. She has read it twelve times.

The side door opens. WALTER NGATA, 50s, prosecutor, tie loosened to half-mast, carries two paper cups and sets one on her table without a word. He lowers himself into the chair opposite, and for a while the only sound is the fan, the clock, the hum of the vending machine in the corridor. She lifts the cup, sniffs it, takes a sip, and winces. He does the same and winces in precisely the same way, and they both look at the cups as if the coffee had betrayed them both equally.

                    WALTER
It's terrible.

                    RHEA
It's honest.

                    WALTER
I wanted to say good trial.

                    RHEA
You won, Walter. You can say anything.

                    WALTER
I know. That's why it's hard to say.

She turns the verdict form face down. He watches her do it. Then he stands, crosses to the jury box, and picks up a cushion that has slid off a seat, patting it back into place as though it matters. She laughs once, without humor, and he turns.

                    WALTER (CONT'D)
What?

                    RHEA
You're tidying.

                    WALTER
Habit.

                    RHEA
Thirty years and you still fix the chairs.

                    WALTER
Someone has to.

                    RHEA
Hector Salas is going away for twenty-five years.

                    WALTER
He was guilty.

                    RHEA
He was nineteen.

                    WALTER
So was the boy in the road.

Silence. The fan ticks. She looks at her hands.

                    RHEA
I know. I stood there while his mother cried, and I thought, I'm on the wrong side of this room.

                    WALTER
You weren't.

                    RHEA
No. I wasn't. Somebody had to be.

He sits again, heavily. Pushes the cup toward the center of the table. She pushes hers to meet it.

                    WALTER
Want to get a drink?

                    RHEA
I want to go home and sleep for a week.

                    WALTER
That's a no.

                    RHEA
That's a ask me again in a month.

                    WALTER
I will.

She stands, gathers her files and her shoes, the verdict form folded into her briefcase. At the door she pauses with a hand on the light switch.

                    RHEA
Walter.

                    WALTER
Yes?

                    RHEA
Fix the chairs on your way out.

She flicks the switch. Half the room goes dark. He sits a moment longer in the remaining light, then rises and begins, one by one, to straighten them.
